  <section class="section" id="what-you-keep" aria-labelledby="h-keep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Security and governance</p>
      <h2 id="h-keep">The logic stays in ABAP; logon, authorizations and data stay SAP's</h2>
      <p class="intro">One ABAP class per app, the business logic in it, no generated artifacts, no tables that describe a screen. Nothing of the app runs in the browser, so there is no client logic to secure. Every rule that applies to ABAP, from code review to the four-eyes principle on transports, applies to an app unchanged.</p>
      <div class="table">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col" class="us">abap2UI5</th><th scope="col">A tool that stores apps as configuration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{keep.map(([k, a, b]) => <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td class="us" data-th="abap2UI5">{a}</td><td data-th="A tool that stores apps as configuration">{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="plain two">
        {governance.map(([icon, h, t]) => <li><Icon name={icon} /><div><b>{h}</b><span>{t}</span></div></li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="callout"><b>No shadow IT.</b> No click-together designer bypasses your development process: every app is a class, under the rules you already have.</p>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/security`}>Security</a>, <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/authorization`}>Authorization</a>, and the <a href={`${GITHUB}/blob/main/docs/security.md`}>security model</a> a security review starts from.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

